window.PhoenixSiteSearchIndex={"generated_at_utc":"2026-11-03T23:25:52.259533Z","count":8,"pages":[{"title":"Dark sky notes","url":"/dark-sky-notes/","description":"Artificial light at night is one of the most useful technologies our species has ever deployed - and one of the least carefully aimed.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Dark sky notes Artificial light at night is one of the most useful technologies our species has ever deployed and one of the least carefully aimed. Artificial light at night is one of the most useful technologies our species has ever deployed and one of the least carefully aimed. When light escapes upward from unshielded fixtures, over bright signage, and building façades, it scatters in the atmosphere and returns as a dome of skyglow that hides the stars. Dark sky notes e1f2a3 Updated 2026 11 03 Introduction This site, Dark Sky Notes, is a set of practical guides to that problem. It is written for three readers at once: the backyard astronomer who wants to understand why the horizon glows orange, the homeowner or facilities manager deciding which fixtures to buy, and the council officer or community volunteer who wants a lighting policy that keeps streets safe without washing out the sky. Illustration 1 Three Buckets of Wasted Light What is actually at stake The costs of wasted light fall into three buckets, each covered by a guide in this series. The sky itself. Skyglow is the cumulative glow produced by light scattered off molecules and aerosols. It is strongest near cities but travels far: observers tens of kilometres from a large metro area still see their zenith brightened by it. The physics of that scattering and the reason the colour of a lamp matters as much as its wattage is covered in How skyglow works . Living things. Night is a habitat. Artificial light alters reproduction, foraging, migration, and orientation across an enormous range of species from sea turtle hatchlings that circle inland toward lit resorts instead of toward the moonlit sea, to the hundreds of millions of birds that migrate at night and are drawn off course by tall lit structures. The evidence base, including experimental work that turned city lights off and watched bird behaviour change within minutes, is reviewed in Wildlife and artificial light . Human sleep and circadian health. The human circadian system is exquisitely sensitive to short wavelength light in the evening. Controlled laboratory studies show that reading on a self lit screen before bed delays melatonin release and shifts the body clock relative to reading a printed book under dim light.[3] Outdoor artificial light at night has been associated with the usual caveats of observational research with disrupted sleep and with elevated risks of hormone related cancers in heavily exposed urban populations.[4][5] The strength and limits of that evidence are laid out in Light, health and sleep. Why No One Can See the Stars Anymore Channel: Be Smart Open on YouTube The reason for optimism Light pollution is unusually fixable, for a pollutant. It produces no legacy residue: turn a fixture off or shield it properly and the harm stops immediately, with no cleanup. The fixes are mostly known and mostly cheap: Shielding. A full cutoff fixture directs light where it is needed and none into the sky. Fixing fixture geometry alone can eliminate most direct upward emission. Right sizing. Many outdoor installations are simply brighter than their task requires. Dimming, trimming hours with timers and motion sensors, and deleting redundant fixtures cut both light and electricity bills. Warmer spectrum. Blue rich white light (4000 K and above) scatters more in the atmosphere and suppresses melatonin more effectively than amber or warm white sources of equal lumen output.[6] Choosing 2700 K or lower, or narrow spectrum amber, reduces both impacts. The practical side from a single porch lamp to a municipal ordinance is worked through in Fixing light pollution , and the places that have gone furthest, certified dark sky reserves and parks where the Milky Way still casts a shadow, are described in Where the dark remains . Illustration 2 Fixing Light Pollution: Three Solutions How to use these guides Each guide is self contained and can be read in any order. If you want one starting point: New to the subject? Begin with What light pollution means , which defines skyglow, glare, trespass, and clutter, and explains how darkness is measured and graded. Planning work on your own property? Go straight to Fixing light pollution and its fixture checklist. Trying to make a case to a council or community association? How skyglow works and Measuring darkness give you the numbers; Fixing light pollution gives you the policy vocabulary. Teaching or writing about the subject? Measuring darkness shows how professional and citizen science measurements are actually made, including the global star count programme whose 2011 2022 results showed visible sky quality degrading by close to a tenth per year.[7] Every claim in these guides is sourced in numbered endnotes to the underlying study or institutional reference, with a Further reading list of verified starting points and short video explainers embedded where they genuinely help. Illustration 3 Rising Light Footprint: 2010s Data A note on honesty and uncertainty Evidence in this field is uneven, and these guides do not flatten that unevenness. Experimental studies lights turned on and off in a controlled way exist for some ecological questions and almost no human health questions. Human outdoor lighting epidemiology is observational, exposure is usually estimated from satellite data that under detects blue rich LED light, and confounding by urbanisation is real. Where the literature is mixed, the guides say so. Light at night is not a poison with a linear dose response curve, and darkness advocacy is not a demand to abolish night lighting; streets, workplaces, and homes need to be lit, and the entire purpose of this site is to light them well. Related guides The guides pair naturally: What light pollution means introduces the vocabulary that How skyglow works uses; Wildlife and artificial light and Light, health and sleep cover the two biological audiences of wasted light; Measuring darkness explains the instruments whose output drives the atlas maps in How skyglow works; Fixing light pollution turns everything into decisions, and Where the dark remains shows the outcome at landscape scale. Huge loss of stars visible in night sky due to light pollution say scientists BBC News Channel: BBC News Open on YouTube Further reading Fabio Falchi and colleagues, \"The new world atlas of artificial night sky brightness\", Science Advances (2016) Fabio Falchi et al., \"The new world atlas of artificial night sky brightness\", Science Advances . [https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1600377 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Fabio Falchi et al., \"The new world atlas of artificial night sky brightness\", Science Advances . https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1600377. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. Christopher Kyba and colleagues, \"Artificially lit surface of Earth at night increasing in radiance and extent\", Science Advances (2017) Christopher C. M. Kyba et al., \"Artificially lit surface of Earth at night increasing in radiance and extent\", Science Advances . [https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1701528 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Christopher C. M. Kyba et al., \"Artificially lit surface of Earth at night increasing in radiance and extent\", Science Advances . https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1701528. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. [DarkSky International, \"Light pollution\" overview] darksky.org Open source on darksky.org. [Globe at Night citizen science night sky campaign] globeatnight.org Open source on globeatnight.org. Endnotes 1. Fabio Falchi et al., \"The new world atlas of artificial night sky brightness\", Science Advances . https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1600377 . Accessed 3 November 2026. 2. Christopher C. M. Kyba et al., \"Artificially lit surface of Earth at night increasing in radiance and extent\", Science Advances . https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1701528 . Accessed 3 November 2026. 3. Anne Marie Chang et al., \"Evening use of light emitting eReaders negatively affects sleep, circadian timing, and next morning alertness\", PNAS . https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1418490112 . Accessed 3 November 2026. 4. Tongyu Wang, Naoko Kaida and Kosuke Kaida, \"Effects of outdoor artificial light at night on human health and behavior: A literature review\", Environmental Pollution . https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envpol.2023.121321 . Accessed 3 November 2026. 5. Ariadna García Saenz et al., \"Evaluating the Association between Artificial Light at Night Exposure and Breast and Prostate Cancer Risk in Spain\", Environmental Health Perspectives . https://doi.org/10.1289/ehp1837 . Accessed 3 November 2026. 6. Christian B. Luginbuhl et al., \"The impact of light source spectral power distribution on sky glow\", Journal of Quantitative Spectroscopy and Radiative Transfer . https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jqsrt.2013.12.004 . Accessed 3 November 2026. 7. Christopher C. M. Kyba et al., \"Citizen scientists report global rapid reductions in the visibility of stars from 2011 to 2022\", Science ."},{"title":"Fixing light pollution","url":"/fixing-light-pollution/","description":"Light pollution is the rare pollutant that stops the moment you stop emitting it.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Fixing light pollution Light pollution is the rare pollutant that stops the moment you stop emitting it. Light pollution is the rare pollutant that stops the moment you stop emitting it. There is no residue, no half life, no remediation contract turn the fixture off, shield it, or change its colour, and the harm ends immediately. The fixes are also mostly cheap, and many of them save money outright. Fixing light pollution 9f2c71 Updated 2026 11 03 The five principles DarkSky International distils responsible outdoor lighting into five principles, each of which maps directly onto the physics and biology covered elsewhere in this series:[1] 1. Useful. Light should serve a clear purpose. Start by asking whether a fixture is needed at all at that location and hour deletion is the cheapest fix and the one no manufacturer will suggest. 2. Targeted. Light should go where it is needed and nowhere else. Full cutoff fixtures emit nothing above the horizontal plane; aimed fixtures put the beam on the path, not the neighbours' windows or the sky. 3. Low level. Use the minimum intensity for the task. Most outdoor installations exceed recommended illuminance by a wide margin; glare and adaptation make over lit spaces feel less safe, not more. 4. Controlled. Light should be on only when needed: motion sensors for low traffic areas, timers, part night schedules, and curfews for decorative and commercial lighting. 5. Warm coloured. Choose the warmest colour that still meets the visual task 2700 K or lower for general use, amber or narrow band for wildlife sensitive sites because short wavelength light drives skyglow and circadian disruption hardest.[2][3] A fixture that satisfies all five is \"dark sky friendly\" in the meaningful sense not a marketing label, but a checkable description: shielded, right sized, timed, warm. Illustration 1 Homeowner Lighting Audit Steps The homeowner's checklist Work through your property in this order; each step takes minutes and compounds with the rest. Audit at night. Walk your property after dark from the pavement and from the neighbours' side. Look for: fixtures visible as bare light sources from off the property (glare and trespass), light striking windows (yours and others'), and any fixture shining upward (pure waste). Shield or replace floodlights. A full cutoff wall pack or shielded porch fixture that lights the door and step not the street, not the sky typically cuts light leaving the property by more than half while improving what you can actually see. Aim and mount. Floodlights belong horizontal or below, mounted at eaves height or lower; every degree of upward tilt buys skyglow and loses task light. Downgrade wattage. Outdoor LEDs are cheap and bright; most residential needs are met by fixtures in the 300 800 lumen range. If you can read a book comfortably under it, it is brighter than a path needs. Add controls. A motion sensor converts an all night 800 lumen fixture into a 3 minute on demand light less energy, less emission, and arguably better security (sudden light draws attention; constant light is wallpaper). Warm it. When replacing, choose 2700 K or 2200 K. At equal lumens on the ground, the warm choice scatters less in the atmosphere and suppresses melatonin less.[2] 5 Principles of Responsible Lighting to Reduce Light Pollution in Texas (Updated v3) Channel: DarkSky Texas Open on YouTube The vocabulary of policy Councils, planners, and community groups arguing for better lighting need these terms in writing: Full cutoff / ULR. A fixture whose uplight ratio (ULR) is zero no light above horizontal. Procurement language should specify ULR ≤ 0% (or the closest standard class) rather than \"dark sky friendly\". Correlated colour temperature (CCT) caps. Policy language increasingly caps street lighting at 3000 K, with 2700 K common; wildlife sensitive zones specify amber (PC amber, 1800 2200 K, or 589 nm low pressure sodium equivalents). The justification is in the skyglow and biology literature, not aesthetics.[2][3] Lighting zones (LZ0 LZ4). A standard framework from darkest natural areas (LZ0 no ambient lighting, national park cores) to high activity urban cores (LZ4), each zone with caps on lumens, ULR, and curfew requirements. Zones let a single ordinance treat a city centre and a rural edge differently without ad hoc negotiation. Curfew hours. A time after which non essential lighting must dim or extinguish (commonly 22:00 or midnight). Curfews are the cheapest large scale intervention available to a municipality. Illuminance, not lumens, for tasks. Specify the measured light level needed on the surface (lux on the path, at the door), and let bidders meet it with the least emitted light this is how procurement avoids a brightness arms race. Illustration 2 Policy Vocabulary for Councils The LED retrofit trap The great practical lesson of the last decade: efficiency is not reduction. When cities replaced sodium streetlights with efficient white LEDs, the satellite record showed upward radiance and the total lit area continuing to grow the energy savings were converted into more and brighter light rather than into dark.[4] Three specific traps recur: Over lighting \"because we can.\" LED efficacy makes it cheap to exceed every illuminance recommendation; specifications must cap lux, not just watts. Blue rich white by default. Early LED stock favoured 4000 K+ for efficacy; 2700 K and PC amber options now exist at small efficiency penalties and large sky and biology gains.[2] Worse glare geometry. High output LEDs in poorly shielded housings concentrate luminance into harsh points pedestrian level glare complaints rose after several retrofits that ignored fixture optics. A retrofit specified with lux caps, 2700 K or lower CCT, full cutoff fixtures, and curfew dimming delivers the energy win without the sky loss. One that only swaps the source makes the problem worse while feeling modern. Illustration 3 LED Retrofit Traps and Fixes Making the case where you live Practical sequencing for local change: start with the inventory (what the council lights, when, at what CCT), because you cannot argue with unmeasured light; ask for trial areas (one street at 2700 K with dimming is a cheap pilot that sceptics can visit); align with money (the controls that cut light cut maintenance and energy budgets); and bring the wildlife hours migration windows and hatchling seasons are when curfews buy the most biology per concession. Related guides The physics behind the spectrum and shielding rules is in How skyglow works; the biological stakes are in Wildlife and artificial light and Light, health and sleep; the units that define lighting zones are explained in Measuring darkness; and the places that took this path far enough to certify are described in Where the dark remains . Further reading DarkSky International, five principles for responsible outdoor lighting DarkSky International, \"Lighting Principles for Responsible Outdoor Lighting\". [https://www.darksky.org/lighting principles/ . Accessed 3 November 2026] DarkSky International, \"Lighting Principles for Responsible Outdoor Lighting\". https://www.darksky.org/lighting principles/. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on darksky.org. Christian B. Luginbuhl, Paul Boley and Donald Davis, \"The impact of light source spectral power distribution on sky glow\", JQSRT (2014) Christian B. Luginbuhl, Paul A. Boley and Donald R. Davis, \"The impact of light source spectral power distribution on sky glow\", Journal of Quantitative Spectroscopy and Radiative Transfer . [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jqsrt.2013.12.004 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Christian B. Luginbuhl, Paul A. Boley and Donald R. Davis, \"The impact of light source spectral power distribution on sky glow\", Journal of Quantitative Spectroscopy and Radiative Transfer . https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jqsrt.2013.12.004. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. Christopher Kyba and colleagues, \"Artificially lit surface of Earth at night increasing in radiance and extent\", Science Advances (2017) Christopher C. M. Kyba, Tamás Küster, Alejandro Sánchez de Miguel, Kimberly Baugh, Andreas Jechow, Franz Hölker and colleagues, \"Artificially lit surface of Earth at night increasing in radiance and extent\", Science Advances . [https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1701528 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Christopher C. M. Kyba, Tamás Küster, Alejandro Sánchez de Miguel, Kimberly Baugh, Andreas Jechow, Franz Hölker and colleagues, \"Artificially lit surface of Earth at night increasing in radiance and extent\", Science Advances . https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1701528. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. Dark Sky Shielded Light Examples Channel: Ryan Parker Colorado roots that run deep Open on YouTube Endnotes 1. DarkSky International, \"Lighting Principles for Responsible Outdoor Lighting\". https://www.darksky.org/lighting principles/ . Accessed 3 November 2026. 2. Christian B. Luginbuhl, Paul A. Boley and Donald R. Davis, \"The"},{"title":"How skyglow works","url":"/how-skyglow-works/","description":"Skyglow looks like magic - a dome of light hanging over a city - but it is bookkeeping, and the same numbers fill the light-level tables of a lighting...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"How skyglow works Skyglow looks like magic a dome of light hanging over a city but it is bookkeeping, and the same numbers fill the light level tables of a lighting... Skyglow looks like magic a dome of light hanging over a city but it is bookkeeping, and the same numbers fill the light level tables of a lighting ordinance. Every lumen a fixture sends above the horizontal either escapes to space or is scattered back down by the molecules and particles in the atmosphere. How skyglow works 8d4e6f Updated 2026 11 03 Debunking the myth about light pollution, and understanding how it affects SNR. Channel: Deep Sky Detail Open on YouTube The two routes upward Light reaches the sky by two routes with different consequences. Direct emission is light that leaves the fixture already aimed upward: the unshielded globe lamp, the wall pack tilted 45 degrees, the advertising sign lit from below, the laser like beam of a floodlight missed by its mounting bracket. Direct emission is pure waste by definition it lights nothing on the ground and it is the component a full cutoff fixture eliminates completely. Fixing fixture geometry is the one intervention with no trade offs at all. Reflected emission is ground reflected light: illumination that does its job on the pavement and then bounces. Roughly a tenth of ground light reflects upward from typical road and roof surfaces; pale surfaces reflect more. Reflected light is the irreducible floor of skyglow you can only reduce it by lighting less and it is why even perfectly shielded cities still glow. The split matters because the two routes respond differently to policy. Shielding laws eliminate the first; dimming, curfews, and lower reflectance trim the second. A city that only shields still glows; a city that only dims still wastes direct uplight. Illustration 1 Skyglow Physics Breakdown What the atmosphere does with it Once light is in the air, two scattering processes send it back down. Rayleigh scattering scattering off molecules of air themselves is strongly wavelength dependent, varying as one over the fourth power of wavelength. Blue light at 450 nanometres scatters roughly two and a half times more than red light at 700 nm. This is the same physics that makes the daytime sky blue, operating at night on city light. Mie scattering scattering off particles: haze, water droplets, dust is much less colour dependent and much stronger in absolute terms when the air is dirty or humid. Mie scattering is why a humid or hazy night over a city glows so much more than a dry, crisp one at identical emission: the particles act as a suspended screen. The practical consequences follow directly. Skyglow is worse in humid climates and in haze. It is worse downwind of urban aerosols. And the part that decides fixture policy because the scattered share of blue light is intrinsically larger, the colour of the lamp changes the skyglow even at identical lumens on the ground . Field and modelling studies of light source spectral power distribution find that blue rich white sources (correlated colour temperature of about 4000 K and above) produce substantially more visible and environmentally effective skyglow than amber or low pressure sodium equivalents delivering the same ground illumination.[1] DarkSky Light Levels Explained Ordinance Templates Channel: DarkSky International Open on YouTube Spectrum: the LED shift For decades the classic urban glow was orange, because low pressure sodium lamps emit almost all their light in a narrow yellow band. That spectrum was terrible for colour rendering but gentle on the sky: narrow band yellow scatters relatively little and is far from the peak sensitivity of the dark adapted human eye (and of many ecological light sensors). The LED transition changed the spectrum. Early municipal LED retrofits, optimised for efficiency and colour rendering, used white light rich in short wavelengths around 450 nm exactly the band that Rayleigh scattering loves, that the dark adapted eye is most sensitive to, and that the human circadian system monitors. Modelling work comparing lamp spectra at matched ground brightness shows the skyglow penalty of blue rich white clearly; amber and warm white (2700 K or lower, and better still narrow band amber) trade a little colour rendering for a large reduction in sky impact.[1] Meanwhile satellite radiance measurements over the LED transition era show total uplight radiance continuing to rise in most of the world efficiency gains converted into more light rather than less with the caveat that the satellite band most used for tracking under detects blue rich sources, so the true growth in blue light is larger than the record shows.[2] How far skyglow travels Skyglow is a regional pollutant, not a local one. A large metro area visibly brightens the sky 50 100 kilometres away; the world atlas computes artificial brightness above natural thresholds across entire countries. Three mechanisms spread it: 1. Scattered light has no loyalty to its source. A photon scattered downward over your dark observing site may have been emitted by a fixture 80 km away. 2. The bright dome competes with the horizon. Even when the zenith over a rural site stays dark, the city dome above the horizon destroys low altitude observing where comets, aurorae, and much deep sky work live. 3. Aerosols amplify distance effects. Particle layers between you and a distant city scatter its light toward you efficiently, which is why distant domes bloom on hazy nights. This geography is why dark sky protection is organised at landscape scale reserves and parks covering hundreds of square kilometres, described in Where the dark remains and why a single well lit distribution warehouse on the edge of a dark region is a regional, not a local, problem. Illustration 2 City Glow vs Natural Sky The honest numbers on how bad it is The atlas work gives the scale of the problem in three statements, each worth quoting carefully. More than 80% of the world's population lives under skies brighter than the natural baseline; for the United States and Europe the figure exceeds 99%. Roughly a third of humanity including about 60% of Europeans and nearly 80% of North Americans cannot see the Milky Way from where they live.[3] And citizen science star counts between 2011 and 2022 found the naked eye sky degrading fast enough to correspond to sky brightness growing on the order of ten percent per year, far faster than satellite only estimates suggested.[4] From mechanism to fix Every rule in the practical guides falls out of this physics: shield fixtures (kill direct emission), dim and trim hours (cut both routes), choose warm or amber spectrum (cut the scattering and the biological effectiveness of what escapes), and control reflections where you can. None of it requires darkness as a lifestyle; it requires light aimed, sized, timed, and coloured for its job the checklist in Fixing light pollution. Related guides The definitions behind this guide are in What light pollution means ; the instruments that turn skyglow into numbers are covered in Measuring darkness; and the biological stakes of the same escaped photons for animals and for human sleep are in Wildlife and artificial light and Light, health and sleep. Illustration 3 Fixing Light Pollution Rules Further reading Christian B. Luginbuhl, Paul Boley and Donald R. Davis, \"The impact of light source spectral power distribution on sky glow\", JQSRT (2014) Christian B. Luginbuhl, Paul A. Boley and Donald R. Davis, \"The impact of light source spectral power distribution on sky glow\", Journal of Quantitative Spectroscopy and Radiative Transfer . [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jqsrt.2013.12.004 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Christian B. Luginbuhl, Paul A. Boley and Donald R. Davis, \"The impact of light source spectral power distribution on sky glow\", Journal of Quantitative Spectroscopy and Radiative Transfer . https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jqsrt.2013.12.004. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. Fabio Falchi and colleagues, \"The new world atlas of artificial night sky brightness\", Science Advances (2016) Fabio Falchi, Pierantonio Cinzano, David Duriscoe, Christopher Kyba, Christopher Elvidge, Kimberly Baugh and colleagues, \"The new world atlas of artificial night sky brightness\", Science Advances . [https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1600377 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Fabio Falchi, Pierantonio Cinzano, David Duriscoe, Christopher Kyba, Christopher Elvidge, Kimberly Baugh and colleagues, \"The new world atlas of artificial night sky brightness\", Science Advances . https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1600377. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. Christopher Kyba and colleagues, \"Artificially lit surface of Earth at night increasing in radiance and extent\", Science Advances (2017) Christopher C. M. Kyba and colleagues, \"Artificially lit surface of Earth at night increasing in radiance and extent\", Science Advances . [https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1701528 ."},{"title":"Light health and sleep","url":"/light-health-and-sleep/","description":"Of all the harms attributed to light pollution, the human-health claims are the most contested - and the most personal, because they arrive in your bedroom.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Light health and sleep Of all the harms attributed to light pollution, the human health claims are the most contested and the most personal, because they arrive in your bedroom. Of all the harms attributed to light pollution, the human health claims are the most contested and the most personal, because they arrive in your bedroom. The honest structure of the evidence has three tiers. At the strongest tier, controlled laboratory experiments show exactly how evening light shifts human circadian biology. Light health and sleep 7f3a58 Updated 2026 11 03 The mechanism: a light sensitive clock in your eye Human circadian timing runs on a master clock in the hypothalamus, entrained each day by light detected not by the rods and cones that build images, but by a separate population of retinal ganglion cells containing the photopigment melanopsin. These cells are most sensitive to short wavelength (blue, roughly 480 nm) light, they respond to the bright end of indoor lighting, and they feed the pineal gland, whose hormone melatonin rises in darkness and signals biological night to every tissue in the body. Two consequences follow. Colour matters : a warm 2700 K lamp suppresses melatonin less than a cool white one of equal brightness, and dim amber hardly at all. Timing matters : the same light that is harmless at noon is a potent clock shifter in the two to three hours before habitual bedtime. Outdoor light trespass through a window is typically far dimmer than a reading lamp but it arrives all night, including the hours when even small doses keep sleep lighter. Illustration 1 From Lab to Bedroom: The Evidence Chain The strongest evidence: controlled experiments The cleanest demonstration comes from a randomised crossover study in which participants read before bed for five nights, once on a light emitting e reader and once with a printed book under dim light. The e reader condition suppressed melatonin by about 55%, delayed melatonin onset by more than an hour, delayed the timing of the body clock, reduced next morning alertness, and shortened subjective sleep quality measured, not asked.[1] This is not a study of streetlights, but it is the proof of mechanism: ordinary evening light, at ordinary indoor intensities, measurably retunes human circadian biology. Everything claimed about outdoor light and sleep leans on this mechanism. The middle tier: outdoor light and population health A 2023 literature review of outdoor artificial light at night and human health surveys the observational record: outdoor ALAN exposure (usually estimated from satellite data) is associated with delayed sleep timing, shortened sleep, worse self reported rest, and in the cancer literature elevated risks of hormone related cancers, with the strongest signals for breast cancer.[2] The single most cited study in that literature used Spanish nighttime satellite data and cancer registry data to estimate outdoor ALAN exposure for individuals: blue enriched outdoor light exposure was associated with breast and prostate cancer risk in dose response fashion.[3] The caveats deserve respect, because they are the difference between evidence and alarmism: Exposure misclassification. Satellite estimates of outdoor light are a crude proxy for the light reaching a sleeping person's retina through curtains, from a window facing an alley or a park. Blue rich LED is under detected by the satellite band most used, biasing the exposure variable.[3] Confounding by urbanisation. Bright neighbourhoods differ from dark ones in noise, shift work, air pollution, income, and health behaviours. The better studies adjust for these; no adjustment is perfect. Ecological scope. Associations are at the level of populations and addresses, not measured bedroom photometry. The defensible summary: there is a plausible mechanism proved in the lab, and consistent but observational population associations that point the same direction. That is a strong basis for precaution in lighting policy and an honest basis for personal action without claiming that outdoor light alone is a proven cause of disease in an individual. How Does Blue Light & Other Light Affect Your Sleep? Dr. Andrew Huberman Channel: Huberman Lab Clips Open on YouTube The weakest tier: claims to treat with care Some widely shared claims do not survive scrutiny. \"Blue light from screens damages your retina\" has no good epidemiological support at ordinary exposure levels the melatonin and clock effects are real; the retinal damage claims are extrapolations from extreme laboratory doses. \"LED streetlights are toxic\" conflates the light (a real circadian concern, dose and spectrum dependent) with electronics and radio control claims that have no evidential basis. The guide's working rule: any claim about light and health should name the dose (illuminance at the eye), the spectrum, and the timing claims that skip all three are usually folklore. What actually helps at night The interventions supported by the mechanism and the trials literature, in rough order of effect per effort: 1. Darken the last two hours. Dim household light and shift to warm sources after dinner; the e reader study shows the payoff of moving in the opposite direction.[1] 2. Fix trespass before fixtures. Blackout curtains, door draught seals on light corridors, and low glare porch fixtures pointed away from windows remove the all night exposure that you cannot otherwise control. 3. Screen time as light, not sin. The issue with late screens is brightness and colour, not the screen per se: night shift modes and dimming help exactly insofar as they cut short wavelength output and retinal illuminance. 4. Morning light closes the loop. Bright light in the first hour after waking anchors the clock and makes the evening discipline easier to hold. For the outdoor side of the problem what a streetlight outside your window means and what a council can do about its spectrum, shielding, and hours the engineering answers are in Fixing light pollution . Illustration 2 Evidence Tiers: Lab vs Streetlights vs Folklore Related guides The spectrum rules here are the same physics that drives skyglow in How skyglow works; the effects of the same escaped light on animals are covered in Wildlife and artificial light ; and Measuring darkness explains how the satellite exposure maps behind the epidemiology are actually built including their blind spots. How Artificial Light at Night Affects Our Circadian Rhythms Dr. Kat Kennedy Channel: DarkSky International Open on YouTube Further reading Anne Marie Chang, Daniel Aeschbach, Jeanne Duffy and Charles Czeisler, \"Evening use of light emitting eReaders negatively affects sleep, circadian timing, and next morning alertness\", PNAS (2014) Anne Marie Chang, Daniel Aeschbach, Jeanne F. Duffy and Charles A. Czeisler, \"Evening use of light emitting eReaders negatively affects sleep, circadian timing, and next morning alertness\", PNAS . [https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1418490112 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Anne Marie Chang, Daniel Aeschbach, Jeanne F. Duffy and Charles A. Czeisler, \"Evening use of light emitting eReaders negatively affects sleep, circadian timing, and next morning alertness\", PNAS . https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1418490112. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. Tongyu Wang, Naoko Kaida and Kosuke Kaida, \"Effects of outdoor artificial light at night on human health and behavior: A literature review\", Environmental Pollution (2023) Tongyu Wang, Naoko Kaida and Kosuke Kaida, \"Effects of outdoor artificial light at night on human health and behavior: A literature review\", Environmental Pollution . [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envpol.2023.121321 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Tongyu Wang, Naoko Kaida and Kosuke Kaida, \"Effects of outdoor artificial light at night on human health and behavior: A literature review\", Environmental Pollution . https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envpol.2023.121321. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. Ariadna García Saenz, Alejandro Sánchez de Miguel, Ariadna Espinosa, Ana Valentin, Nuria Aragonés, Javier Llorca and colleagues, \"Evaluating the Association between Artificial Light at Night Exposure and Breast and Prostate Cancer Risk in Spain\", Environmental Health Perspectives (2018) Ariadna García Saenz, Alejandro Sánchez de Miguel, Ariadna Espinosa, Ana Valentin, Nuria Aragonés, Javier Llorca and colleagues, \"Evaluating the Association between Artificial Light at Night Exposure and Breast and Prostate Cancer Risk in Spain\", Environmental Health Perspectives . [https://doi.org/10.1289/ehp1837 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Ariadna García Saenz, Alejandro Sánchez de Miguel, Ariadna Espinosa, Ana Valentin, Nuria Aragonés, Javier Llorca and colleagues, \"Evaluating the Association between Artificial Light at Night Exposure and Breast and Prostate Cancer Risk in Spain\", Environmental Health Perspectives . https://doi.org/10.1289/ehp1837. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. Illustration 3 Nighttime"},{"title":"Measuring darkness","url":"/measuring-darkness/","description":"Every argument in the dark-sky world eventually becomes a number: this site is 21.4, that city is 17.8, the atlas says 80%, the star counts say ten percent a...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Measuring darkness Every argument in the dark sky world eventually becomes a number: this site is 21.4, that city is 17.8, the atlas says 80%, the star counts say ten percent a... Every argument in the dark sky world eventually becomes a number: this site is 21.4, that city is 17.8, the atlas says 80%, the star counts say ten percent a year. Numbers make claims testable but only if you know how each number was produced and what it can and cannot tell you. Measuring darkness 4e6d92 Updated 2026 11 03 The unit: magnitudes per square arcsecond Sky brightness in this field is usually quoted in magnitudes per square arcsecond (mag/arcsec²): the apparent magnitude of a patch of sky one square arcsecond in size. The scale is inverted larger numbers mean darker skies. Anchors worth memorising: a natural pristine sky measures about 21.9 22.0; a good rural dark site 21.0 21.8; a rural suburban transition 20 21; a bright suburb 18 19; an urban centre 16 17.5. Each whole step of one magnitude is a factor of about 2.5 in brightness, so a city at 17.0 is roughly 100 times brighter than a pristine site at 22.0 the difference between a handful of stars and the Milky Way casting shadows. The subtlety that trips up newcomers: the human eye's naked eye limiting magnitude is related to this number but not linearly, because the eye adapts and integrates differently. Two skies 0.5 mag/arcsec² apart can differ dramatically in what you can see, and humidity can shift a single site by more than that in one night. Hence the professional's rule: one reading is anecdote; a monitored site is data. Illustration 1 Tools for Measuring Darkness Ground instruments Sky quality meters (SQM). The handheld workhorse: a photodiode behind a fish eye lens, reading zenith brightness in a few seconds, accurate to a few hundredths of a magnitude when used with care (dark adapted, away from obstructions, multiple readings averaged). Their broadband detector does not record spectrum, which matters when comparing amber lit and white lit towns two sites can read identically while looking nothing alike. All sky cameras. A camera with a fish eye lens and photometric calibration produces a brightness map of the entire sky dome, from which researchers derive integrated indicators the natural sky luminance normalised zenith, the brightness weighted average of the dome, the horizon direction maximum. The methodology literature shows how such maps are calibrated and which indicators are robust to camera and site differences, which is what makes cross site comparisons defensible.[1] A methods and challenges review covering the whole toolbox meters, cameras, drones, and satellites is the standard reference for what each instrument measures and its systematic errors.[2] DSLR cameras. A consumer camera on a tripod with fixed settings, calibrated against star fields, gives repeatable sky brightness for the price of a camera many people already own the basis of most academic citizen science campaigns. How Dark Are Your Skies, Really? An Astrophotographer’s Perspective. Channel: StarScaper Open on YouTube Satellites: the view from orbit and its blind spot The satellite series behind most world maps (including the famous light pollution globes) is sensitive in a broad visible to near infrared band. It measures upward radiance light leaving the ground not sky brightness at any observer. From these radiance data, the atlas models compute artificial sky brightness at ground level; the modelling chain and its validation are documented in the world atlas papers.[3] The blind spot matters and is increasingly cited: the satellite band under weights the short wavelength blue light that LED street lighting emits heavily and that both scatters most in the atmosphere ( How skyglow works ) and matters most biologically ( Wildlife and artificial light ; Light, health and sleep). A city that swapped sodium for blue rich LED can look dimmer to the satellite while its sky got brighter. Radiance vs extent analysis of the satellite record over the LED transition era shows global upward light still increasing about 2% per year in radiance and extent in the early 2010s with the true increase in visually and biologically effective light probably larger than the satellite record shows.[4] Citizen science: Globe at Night and the star count trend The Globe at Night programme asks participants to match the constellation they see (Orion, Leo, Cygnus, Crux, depending on season and hemisphere) against magnitude charts, recording the faintest star visible a naked eye measurement with defined error characteristics, repeated worldwide for over a decade. The 2023 analysis of nearly 45,000 such observations from 2011 to 2022 found that visible sky quality is degrading fast: expressed as sky brightness, the global aggregate corresponds to an increase of about 9.6% per year far faster than ground based satellite estimates implied with the fastest degradation in North America and Europe, where most measurements come from and where most people live under already bright skies.[5] That number deserves its caveats: observations cluster where observers are (urban, wealthy, northern hemisphere skies), naked eye limiting magnitude is a coarse ordinal scale, and annual weather and volcanic aerosols move individual years. The study's authors addressed the major biases, and no plausible correction erases the signal: the star filled sky is disappearing from human experience on a timescale of years, not centuries. Illustration 2 Sky Brightness Scale Ranges How to measure your own sky, honestly A defensible personal baseline takes about fifteen minutes: 1. Pick a zenith spot with a clear horizon, note date, time, moon phase, and weather. 2. Let your eyes dark adapt ten minutes; no phone in that window. 3. Find the seasonal campaign constellation and match it to a Globe at Night magnitude chart; record the limiting magnitude (naked eye) the programme's instructions walk you through it.[6] 4. Optionally average 3 5 SQM readings taken a minute apart. 5. Repeat monthly, same site, same conditions class the value is in the series, not the number. If you are choosing where to observe or holiday, the interactive maps built from the atlas data are a legitimate first filter[3][7] cross check them against recent local measurements, because maps age faster than skies in either direction. Explanatory illustration 3 Related guides What light pollution means defines the scales in words; How skyglow works explains why the blue light blind spot matters physically; Fixing light pollution turns measurement into policy (lighting zones are defined in the same mag/arcsec² units), and Where the dark remains is about the places whose measurements are the best on Earth. Further reading Andreas Hänel, Thomas Posch, Salvador Ribas, Martin Aubé, Dan Duriscoe, Andreas Jechow and colleagues, \"Measuring night sky brightness: methods and challenges\", JQSRT (2018) Andreas Hänel, Thomas Posch, Salvador Ribas, Martin Aubé, Dan Duriscoe, Andreas Jechow and colleagues, \"Measuring night sky brightness: methods and challenges\", Journal of Quantitative Spectroscopy and Radiative Transfer . [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jqsrt.2017.09.008 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Andreas Hänel, Thomas Posch, Salvador Ribas, Martin Aubé, Dan Duriscoe, Andreas Jechow and colleagues, \"Measuring night sky brightness: methods and challenges\", Journal of Quantitative Spectroscopy and Radiative Transfer . https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jqsrt.2017.09.008. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. Dan Duriscoe, \"Photometric indicators of visual night sky quality derived from all sky brightness maps\", JQSRT (2016) Dan Duriscoe, \"Photometric indicators of visual night sky quality derived from all sky brightness maps\", Journal of Quantitative Spectroscopy and Radiative Transfer . [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jqsrt.2016.02.022 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Dan Duriscoe, \"Photometric indicators of visual night sky quality derived from all sky brightness maps\", Journal of Quantitative Spectroscopy and Radiative Transfer . https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jqsrt.2016.02.022. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. Christopher Kyba and colleagues, \"Citizen scientists report global rapid reductions in the visibility of stars from 2011 to 2022\", Science (2023) Christopher C. M. Kyba, Yıldırım Altıntaş, Constance Walker and Mark Newhouse, \"Citizen scientists report global rapid reductions in the visibility of stars from 2011 to 2022\", Science . [https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abq7781 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Christopher C. M. Kyba, Yıldırım Altıntaş, Constance Walker and Mark Newhouse, \"Citizen scientists report global rapid reductions in the visibility of stars from 2011 to 2022\", Science . https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abq7781. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. [Globe at Night citizen science campaign instructions] Globe at Night, campaign instructions and magnitude charts."},{"title":"What light pollution means","url":"/what-light-pollution-means/","description":"Light pollution\" sounds like a single thing, but it is a family of four distinct problems that happen to share a cause: light going where it is not wanted.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"What light pollution means Light pollution\" sounds like a single thing, but it is a family of four distinct problems that happen to share a cause: light going where it is not wanted. Light pollution\" sounds like a single thing, but it is a family of four distinct problems that happen to share a cause: light going where it is not wanted. The four are usually named skyglow, glare, light trespass, and clutter. What light pollution means 5b7c9d Updated 2026 11 03 Introduction This guide defines each term the way researchers and lighting professionals use it, explains how darkness is graded and measured in plain numbers, and clears up the most common confusion: the difference between the light you can see being wasted and the light the atmosphere returns to you as a glowing dome. The four problems Skyglow Skyglow is the brightening of the night sky itself. Some of it is natural airglow, scattered starlight, and zodiacal light exist even at the darkest sites but the dominant form over cities is artificial: light from ground sources scattered by air molecules and airborne particles, returning to the ground as a diffuse glow. Skyglow is why the urban sky looks grey or orange instead of black, why only the brightest stars survive, and why the Milky Way is invisible to more than a third of the world's people.[1] Its physics and the surprising importance of the colour of the lamp is the subject of How skyglow works . Glare Glare is light that enters the eye directly and reduces your ability to see. It comes in two flavours that matter outdoors: disability glare , which scatters inside the eye and washes out the scene (an unshielded floodlight above a driveway, seen head on, can make the intruder behind it invisible), and discomfort glare , which is merely unpleasant but fatiguing. Glare is a safety problem masquerading as a safety feature: beyond a point, more brightness makes people feel safer while actually seeing less. SKYGLOW What is Light Pollution? Channel: wm Open on YouTube Light trespass Light trespass is light that crosses a property boundary the neighbour's porch lamp painting a stripe across your bedroom ceiling, a car park fixture lighting an apartment two floors up. It is the form of light pollution most likely to reach human sleep, because windows are where outdoor light becomes indoor light. Trespass is also the easiest to legislate, because a boundary makes the harm concrete. Clutter and overlighting Clutter is the visual chaos of competing bright signs and fixtures; overlighting is simply more light than the task needs. Both matter because they are the upstream cause of the other three: unshielded, redundant, and excessive installations are what send light into eyes, windows, and the sky. The world night sky brightness atlas found that the artificially lit area of the planet keeps expanding and that radiance the brightness of that lit area rose in most countries studied, evidence that efficient LED lighting has so far been used to light more, not less.[2] How dark is \"dark\"? Grading the night Because \"you should see my sky\" is not evidence, observers grade darkness in numbers. Three scales dominate. Naked eye limiting magnitude. The faintest star a dark adapted eye can see straight up, expressed in astronomical magnitude (each magnitude is about 2.5 times fainter). Under a pristine sky, magnitude 6.5 or fainter; under bright urban skyglow, perhaps 3.5 or worse a loss of tens of visible stars out of thousands. The Bortle scale. A nine class scale from 1 (pristine, astronomer's gold: the zodiacal band casts shadows, the Southern sky glows faintly) to 9 (inner city: the sky is grey or orange, fewer than a dozen stars visible). Bortle classes are defined by observable phenomena whether the Milky Way casts shadows, whether M31 is visible which makes them robust but coarse. Most suburban dwellers live under class 7 to 9; most have never seen anything darker than class 5. Instrument magnitudes per square arcsecond. Sky quality meters and all sky cameras report sky brightness in magnitudes per square arcsecond (mag/arcsec²). Rough anchors: 22.0 is an excellent natural dark site, 19.5 20.5 is a good rural sky, 18 is a bright suburb, and 16 17 is a city centre. These are the numbers used by the world atlas and by every serious monitoring programme, and the conversion between them and what your eye perceives is not linear a sky \"twice as bright\" in physical units can look dramatically worse to a dark adapted observer.[3] Archive evidence In the photo there is one Perseid, Milky Way and Andromega galaxy and light pollution on the horizon Luhasoo bog in Estonia Martin Mark · Own work · 2015 08 12 00:45:09 · 45591215 Archival image related to In the photo there is one Perseid, Milky Way and Andromega galaxy and light pollution on the horizon Luhasoo bog in Estonia. CC BY SA 4.0 · View original record The map is not the sky Two cautions keep this vocabulary honest. First, satellite maps of light pollution the familiar glowing globe images measure light emitted upward at the wavelengths the satellite sees, not sky brightness as experienced on the ground. Modern blue rich white LEDs are under represented in the most used satellite band, which means some of the most disruptive lighting is literally dimmer on the map than in the sky.[4] The atlas models correct for much of this, but any single map is an estimate, not a measurement of your backyard. Second, sky brightness varies with weather, season, and airborne particles a humid night glows more than a dry one at the same emission. A single reading is anecdote; a series of readings, at the same site, over months, is data. That is exactly the logic of the citizen science star counts described in Measuring darkness , which aggregated nearly a decade of observations and found visible sky quality declining globally by close to ten percent per year in the places people actually measured.[5] What light pollution means not Two honest caveats. Darkness advocacy is not a demand for dark streets: the goal of every serious dark sky organisation is well directed light, with fixtures that put light on the ground where people need it a goal that aligns with safety, not against it.[6] And not every bright light is pollution: aviation, maritime, and some workplace lighting are legally or practically required. The question this site asks throughout is narrower and more useful is the light doing its job, and is any of it escaping where it does harm? Fixing light pollution works through that door. Related guides The definitions above set up the rest of the series: How skyglow works takes skyglow into the physics of scattering and lamp spectrum, Measuring darkness shows how the numbers in this guide are actually produced, and Fixing light pollution turns the four problems into fixture level and policy level decisions. The Strange Scourge of Light Pollution Channel: SciShow Open on YouTube Further reading Fabio Falchi and colleagues, \"The new world atlas of artificial night sky brightness\", Science Advances (2016) Fabio Falchi, Pierantonio Cinzano, David Duriscoe, Christopher Kyba, Christopher Elvidge, Kimberly Baugh and colleagues, \"The new world atlas of artificial night sky brightness\", Science Advances . [https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1600377 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Fabio Falchi, Pierantonio Cinzano, David Duriscoe, Christopher Kyba, Christopher Elvidge, Kimberly Baugh and colleagues, \"The new world atlas of artificial night sky brightness\", Science Advances . https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1600377. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. [DarkSky International, \"Light pollution\" overview] DarkSky International, \"Light pollution\" and responsible lighting principles. https://www.darksky.org/light pollution/. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on darksky.org. Christopher Kyba and colleagues, \"Citizen scientists report global rapid reductions in the visibility of stars from 2011 to 2022\", Science (2023) Christopher C. M. Kyba, Yıldırım Altıntaş, Constance Walker and Mark Newhouse, \"Citizen scientists report global rapid reductions in the visibility of stars from 2011 to 2022\", Science . [https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abq7781 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Christopher C. M. Kyba, Yıldırım Altıntaş, Constance Walker and Mark Newhouse, \"Citizen scientists report global rapid reductions in the visibility of stars from 2011 to 2022\", Science . https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abq7781. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. Endnotes 1. Fabio Falchi, Pierantonio Cinzano, David Duriscoe, Christopher Kyba, Christopher Elvidge, Kimberly Baugh and colleagues, \"The new world atlas of artificial night sky brightness\", Science Advances . https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1600377 . Accessed 3 November 2026. 2. Christopher C. M. Kyba, Tamás Küster, Alejandro Sánchez de Miguel, Kimberly Baugh, Andreas Jechow, Franz Hölker and colleagues, \"Artificially lit surface of Earth at night increasing in radiance"},{"title":"Where the dark remains","url":"/where-the-dark-remains/","description":"Under the world atlas, darkness survives as islands: mountaintops above the haze, deserts far from megacities, small islands, and a few carefully managed...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Where the dark remains Under the world atlas, darkness survives as islands: mountaintops above the haze, deserts far from megacities, small islands, and a few carefully managed... Under the world atlas, darkness survives as islands: mountaintops above the haze, deserts far from megacities, small islands, and a few carefully managed rural regions. Where the dark remains 3d7e84 Updated 2026 11 03 Why darkness needs geography Skyglow is regional. A single bright source brightens the sky for tens of kilometres, and the dome of a major metro area reaches 100 km and more; the physics is laid out in How skyglow works . No patch of land smaller than a landscape stays dark when its surroundings glow which is why darkness is protected through reserves, parks, and sanctuaries measured in hundreds of square kilometres, with lighting rules binding across the whole territory, rather than through isolated \"dark corners\". The brightest purity sites that remain high, dry, remote places like the Atacama, the high Himalaya, the southwestern US deserts, parts of central Australia and southern Africa combine distance from cities with low aerosol loads: the same conditions professional observatories seek. Illustration 1 IDSP Certification Tiers Certification: the International Dark Sky Places programme The best known framework for recognising and protecting dark areas is the International Dark Sky Places (IDSP) programme, run by DarkSky International. It certifies several categories: International Dark Sky Parks publicly or privately protected land with exceptional starry nights and nocturnal habitat, committed to lighting management, public education, and monitoring. International Dark Sky Reserves a dark \"core\" zone (often an observatory landscape) surrounded by a populated buffer where communities commit to lighting controls; the reserve model is explicitly regional. International Dark Sky Sanctuaries the most remote, darkest sites, typically with the strictest restrictions on access and lighting. International Dark Sky Communities towns and cities that have adopted comprehensive lighting ordinances and public education, proving the programme is not only for wilderness: a municipality of tens of thousands can qualify by lighting well . Certification is not an award for pre existing darkness; it is a verified management commitment an inventory of existing lighting, a retrofit plan with schedules, outdoor lighting standards for new development, ongoing sky brightness monitoring, and public interpretation. Places have lost certification when commitments lapsed, which is the point: the label is enforceable in reputation if not in law. Light Pollution and Astronomy Channel: DarkSky International Open on YouTube What protection looks like in practice The measures that hold a dark landscape dark are the same as the Fixing light pollution checklist, applied at scale and enforced in planning: 1. Ordinance backed lighting standards full cutoff fixtures, CCT caps (commonly ≤2700 K, amber in sensitive cores), lumen budgets, and curfews across the whole territory. 2. Retrofit programmes streetlight and commercial retrofits with schedules and financing, because goodwill without deadlines does not change stock. 3. Monitoring regular sky brightness measurement (the instruments are described in Measuring darkness ) so that regressions are detected in data, not anecdotes. 4. Gateway economies communities that certify usually lean into astro tourism: dark sky festivals, star parties, guided night walks, astronomy friendly accommodation (red light corridors, curtain standards). The economic return gives residents a stake in the rules, which is what makes the rules durable. Person Grand Canyon National Park International Dark Sky Park Certification Ceremony 48128180961 Grand Canyon NPS · Wikimedia Commons · 2019 06 22 11:50:30 · 88936428 Archival image related to Grand Canyon National Park International Dark Sky Park Certification Ceremony 48128180961. CC BY 2.0 · View original record Why astronomy concentrated there Every great modern observatory sits in a certified or de facto dark sky landscape Chile's Atacama, the Canary Islands' La Palma, Hawaii's Mauna Kea, the southwestern US, Namibia, outback Australia. Observatory siting drove some of the earliest lighting ordinances: communities near professional telescopes adopted codes decades before \"light pollution\" was a public term, because a telescope's science is directly priced in sky brightness. The astronomy economy also anchors protection politically observatories bring high skill jobs and international investment, and their light budgets give regional planners a quantified reason to say no to a lit development. The same satellite record that tracks the world's brightening (a 2% per year radiance growth in the early 2010s, with lit area expanding) shows why those anchors matter: everything not protected has been getting brighter.[2] Visiting a dark place well Etiquette is mostly physics and courtesy: Arrive before dark and set up while you can still see; astro sites are dark. Red light only after dusk a dim red torch preserves everyone's dark adaptation; white headlamps reset an entire viewing field's eyes for twenty minutes. Handle headlights and interiors parking orientation, dome lights off, screens dimmed and reddened; a single white reversing light can undo a star party. Follow the lodging rules at dark sky accommodation: curtains closed, warm porch light, no floodlights \"just for a moment\". Leave the site as dark as you found it no landscape lighting, no lanterns, no \"one photo with the flash\". What you get in return is the experience the atlas numbers put out of reach for a third of humanity: the Milky Way bright enough to cast shadows, the zodiacal band, airglow, and thousands of stars the sky that every human culture before the twentieth century took for granted, and that citizen science star counts now measure disappearing at close to ten percent a year where people live.[3] Illustration 2 Fixing Light Pollution Checklist The strategic point Dark places do more than serve astronomers and holidaymakers. They are control sites for climate and atmospheric science, refuges for light sensitive ecology ( Wildlife and artificial light ), and living demonstrations for every policy conversation: a council sceptical of a 2700 K cap can be brought to a reserve and shown what its own sky could look like a ridge away. Protection at the landscape scale is the endgame of every guide in this series the vocabulary of What light pollution means , the physics of How skyglow works, the measurements of Measuring darkness, and the rules of Fixing light pollution, all applied across a whole territory and kept honest by monitoring. What Did Ancient Humans See In The Night Sky That You Can’t? Channel: MindPeek Open on YouTube Related guides This guide closes the series: it assumes the definitions of What light pollution means, the scattering physics of How skyglow works, the instruments of Measuring darkness, and the fixture and ordinance rules of Fixing light pollution. Illustration 3 Visiting Etiquette Rules Further reading Fabio Falchi and colleagues, \"The new world atlas of artificial night sky brightness\", Science Advances (2016) Fabio Falchi, Pierantonio Cinzano, David Duriscoe, Christopher Kyba, Christopher Elvidge, Kimberly Baugh and colleagues, \"The new world atlas of artificial night sky brightness\", Science Advances . [https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1600377 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Fabio Falchi, Pierantonio Cinzano, David Duriscoe, Christopher Kyba, Christopher Elvidge, Kimberly Baugh and colleagues, \"The new world atlas of artificial night sky brightness\", Science Advances . https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1600377. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. Christopher Kyba and colleagues, \"Artificially lit surface of Earth at night increasing in radiance and extent\", Science Advances (2017) Christopher C. M. Kyba, Tamás Küster, Alejandro Sánchez de Miguel, Kimberly Baugh, Andreas Jechow, Franz Hölker and colleagues, \"Artificially lit surface of Earth at night increasing in radiance and extent\", Science Advances . [https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1701528 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Christopher C. M. Kyba, Tamás Küster, Alejandro Sánchez de Miguel, Kimberly Baugh, Andreas Jechow, Franz Hölker and colleagues, \"Artificially lit surface of Earth at night increasing in radiance and extent\", Science Advances . https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1701528. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. Christopher Kyba and colleagues, \"Citizen scientists report global rapid reductions in the visibility of stars from 2011 to 2022\", Science (2023) Christopher C. M. Kyba, Yıldırım Altıntaş, Constance Walker and Mark Newhouse, \"Citizen scientists report global rapid reductions in the visibility of stars from 2011 to 2022\", Science . [https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abq7781 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Christopher C. M."},{"title":"Wildlife and artificial light","url":"/wildlife-and-artificial-light/","description":"Night is a habitat the way a forest or a tidepool is: a stable environment that half the species on Earth are adapted to exploit.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Wildlife and artificial light Night is a habitat the way a forest or a tidepool is: a stable environment that half the species on Earth are adapted to exploit. Night is a habitat the way a forest or a tidepool is: a stable environment that half the species on Earth are adapted to exploit. Artificial light at night (often abbreviated ALAN in the research literature) edits that habitat abruptly, locally, and in a spectrum and timing that natural selection never previewed. Wildlife and artificial light 2c9b41 Updated 2026 11 03 Why night matters biologically Three facts about nocturnal biology explain most of the damage. Circadian and circannual clocks are light set. Nearly every organism uses light, especially the blue end of twilight, as the primary time signal for daily and seasonal rhythms. Night lighting delivers a fake dawn cue at the wrong hour. Darkness is a resource. For prey species, night is cover; for nocturnal predators, it is a workspace. Light changes the terms of a predator prey arms race instantly favouring, depending on the system, either the predator that hunts by the new light or the prey that avoids it. Natural light cues are faint and directional. Sea turtles find the sea by moving toward the bright, low horizon over the water; moths navigate by maintaining a constant angle to the distant moon; migratory birds calibrate by star patterns and by the horizon's polarised twilight. These systems work because natural cues are dim and far away. A porch lamp, a streetlight, or a lit tower is bright and near it outshouts the cue the animal's navigation was built around. Illustration 1 Biological Clocks Disrupted Case study: sea turtle hatchlings On a natural beach, hatchling loggerhead and green turtles emerge from the nest at night and crawl toward the brighter horizon over the ocean, where starlight and moonlight reflect off the surf. Experimental work as early as 2007 demonstrated the mechanism cleanly: hatchlings exposed to artificial lighting orient toward it and away from the sea, and the disorientation persists as long as the light dominates their field of view.[2] On developed coastlines the result is lethal and well documented: hatchlings crawl inland toward dune lighting, roadways, and pool decks, dying of exhaustion, dehydration, predation, or traffic. A 2023 review of coastal development impacts on sea turtle conservation identifies unshielded beachfront lighting as a leading, and fixable, driver of hatchling mortality, with lighting ordinances and amber narrow spectrum fixtures as the demonstrated remedies.[3] The case is instructive because the fix works: Florida style lighting codes (shield fixtures, low mount, long wavelength lamps, curfews during hatchling season) measurably reduce disorientation. Wildlife lighting is one of the few areas where the engineering guidance is settled. Case study: night migrating birds Hundreds of millions of birds migrate at night, navigating partly by star patterns and magnetism, and drawn for reasons still under study to bright artificial lights. The definitive experimental demonstration comes from New York City's Tribute in Light memorial: two vertical columns of intense light over lower Manhattan. Radar and acoustic monitoring around the installation found that when the beams were on, birds aggregated in high densities within the lit volume, circled, called frequently, and slowed their migration; when operators shut the beams off for even short periods, the birds resumed normal migration within minutes. The installation now runs with automated shut offs triggered by radar detection of high bird densities a rare example of a harm being switched off literally on demand.[4] The same attraction kills at scale: tall lit structures and bright low buildings cause fatal collisions, which is why \"lights out\" campaigns during spring and autumn migration voluntary building darkening programmes in dozens of North American and European cities are among the most cost effective conservation actions available, costing building operators almost nothing. The Dark Side of Light: How Light Pollution threatens wildlife! Channel: Leo's Animal Planet Open on YouTube Case study: insects Insects are in broad decline, and light is one of the named drivers. A widely cited 2020 review gathered experimental and observational evidence across taxa moths drawn to lamps and killed or exhausted, mayflies whose mating swarms are disrupted by bridges of light, dung beetles that lose their celestial orientation compass and argued that light pollution acts on insects through attraction, repulsion, and the re engineering of entire nocturnal communities, interacting with the better known drivers of habitat loss and pesticides.[5] The review's practical warning is that even rural, low density lighting can matter: a single farmyard lamp restructures the local insect community in a radius of metres to tens of metres, and the effect compounds along every road and yard. Plants and the wider community Light also reaches organisms that cannot move away. An experimental and synthesis programme on wild plants found artificial night light altering flowering phenology, bud burst, leaf retention, and herbivore interactions effects that propagate to the insects and birds that depend on those timing cues.[6] The mechanistic review literature emphasises that these are community level reorganisations, not just individual stress: light changes when plants flower, when pollinators visit, when predators hunt, and how species assemble.[1] Illustration 2 Tribute in Light vs Nature What the evidence supports in practice Four rules carry the strongest evidence for wildlife sensitive lighting: 1. Spectrum first. Long wavelength (amber, 589 nm or longer) light at matched illuminance attracts and disrupts markedly less than broad white or blue rich light across turtles, insects, and many birds. This aligns exactly with the skyglow physics in How skyglow works . 2. Shield and lower. A fixture that emits nothing above horizontal and is mounted low puts light on the task and removes the bright cue from the horizon and the sky. 3. Time limit. Motion sensors, part night dimming, and seasonal curfews (hatchling season, migration nights) remove the harm during exactly the hours it matters. 4. Kill redundant light. The cheapest fix is deletion many wildlife conflicting installations serve no task at all after midnight. Illustration 3 Four Rules for Wildlife Lighting Related guides The physics behind the spectrum rule is explained in What light pollution means and How skyglow works; the same short wavelength sensitivity that disrupts animals disrupts human sleep, covered in Light, health and sleep; and the fixture level implementation of these rules is the checklist in Fixing light pollution . Further reading Travis Longcore and Catherine Rich, \"Ecological light pollution\", Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment (2004) Travis Longcore and Catherine Rich, \"Ecological light pollution\", Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment . [https://doi.org/10.1890/1540 9295(2004)002 002)[0191:ELP]2.0.CO;2. Accessed 3 November 2026] Travis Longcore and Catherine Rich, \"Ecological light pollution\", Frontiers in Ecology and the Environment . https://doi.org/10.1890/1540 9295(2004)002[0191:ELP]2.0.CO;2. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. Kevin J. Gaston, Jonathan Bennie, Thomas W. Davies and John Hopkins, \"The ecological impacts of nighttime light pollution: a mechanistic appraisal\", Biological Reviews (2013) Kevin J. Gaston, Jonathan Bennie, Thomas W. Davies and John Hopkins, \"The ecological impacts of nighttime light pollution: a mechanistic appraisal\", Biological Reviews . [https://doi.org/10.1111/brv.12036 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Kevin J. Gaston, Jonathan Bennie, Thomas W. Davies and John Hopkins, \"The ecological impacts of nighttime light pollution: a mechanistic appraisal\", Biological Reviews . https://doi.org/10.1111/brv.12036. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. Adam S. Owens, Pearse Cochard, Juliane Durrant, Bryony Farnworth, Elizabeth K. Perkin, Brett Seymoure and colleagues, \"Light pollution is a driver of insect declines\", Biological Conservation (2020) Adam S. Owens, Pearse Cochard, Juliane Durrant, Bryony Farnworth, Elizabeth K. Perkin, Brett Seymoure and colleagues, \"Light pollution is a driver of insect declines\", Biological Conservation . [https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biocon.2019.108259 . Accessed 3 November 2026] Adam S. Owens, Pearse Cochard, Juliane Durrant, Bryony Farnworth, Elizabeth K. Perkin, Brett Seymoure and colleagues, \"Light pollution is a driver of insect declines\", Biological Conservation . https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biocon.2019.108259. Accessed 3 November 2026 Open source on doi.org. Light Pollution Harms Sea Turtles Channel: Mote Marine Laboratory & Aquarium Open on YouTube Endnotes 1. Kevin J. Gaston, Jonathan Bennie, Thomas W. Davies and John Hopkins, \"The ecological impacts of nighttime light"}]};
